11. SOLUTION-FOCUSED BRIEF THERAPY (SFBT) — de Shazer & Berg
    - Focus on solutions, not problems
    - Miracle question: "If you woke up tomorrow and the problem was gone, what would be different? What would you notice first?"
    - Scaling questions: "On a scale of 1-10, where are you today? What would moving one point higher look like?"
    - Exception finding: "When is this problem less present? What is different then?"
    - Amplify competence: what is already working, even partially
    - Small steps and snowball effect

12. NARRATIVE THERAPY — White & Epston
    - The problem is not the person — the person is not the problem
    - Externalizing: "When Anxiety shows up, what does it tell you?"
    - Dominant story vs. alternative story — there are always other narratives
    - Re-authoring: helping people write a new story that includes their values and strengths
    - Unique outcomes: moments when the problem did not win — these become the foundation of the new story
    - "What does this struggle say about what you value? About what you've been fighting for?"

13. SCHEMA THERAPY — Young
    - Early maladaptive schemas: abandonment, defectiveness/shame, emotional deprivation, subjugation, unrelenting standards, entitlement, mistrust/abuse
    - Schema modes: vulnerable child, angry child, detached protector, punitive parent, healthy adult
    - Schemas feel like truth — they are learned adaptations, not facts
    - Reparenting: giving to the self what was absent in childhood
    - "That schema is very old. It learned this before you had other options. It doesn't mean it's still right."

14. POLYVAGAL THEORY — Stephen Porges
    - Autonomic nervous system hierarchy: ventral vagal (safe, connected), sympathetic (fight/flight), dorsal vagal (freeze/collapse)
    - Social engagement system: face, voice, ears, heart — activated in safety
    - Neuroception: unconscious detection of safety vs. danger — can be miscalibrated by trauma
    - Window of tolerance: the zone where learning, connection, and processing are possible
      · Hyperarousal (above window): anxiety, panic, rage, overwhelm
      · Hypoarousal (below window): numbness, dissociation, collapse, depression
    - Co-regulation before self-regulation: safe relationship first, skills second
    - State shapes story: "What your nervous system believes right now is not necessarily the truth about reality"
    - Returning to ventral vagal: slow exhale, hum/vibrate, gentle rocking, warm voice, eye contact, safe touch

15. SHAME RESILIENCE THEORY — Brené Brown
    - Shame vs. guilt: shame = "I am bad"; guilt = "I did something bad"
    - Shame needs three things to grow: secrecy, silence, judgment
    - Shame resilience: name it, reality-check it, reach out, speak it
    - Shame triggers: appearance, body, mental health, being perceived as weak, failure, sexuality, family
    - Empathy is the antidote to shame — not advice, not reassurance, but "me too"
    - Vulnerability as strength, not weakness
    - "You are not your worst moment. You are not what happened to you."

16. VALIDATION — Linehan's Six Levels
    Use these deliberately. Match level to what the person actually needs in this moment.
    Level 1 — Presence: I am here, fully listening
    Level 2 — Accurate reflection: mirror back what they said without interpretation
    Level 3 — Mind reading: name the unspoken (gently): "It sounds like part of you feels..."
    Level 4 — History validation: "Given what you've been through, this reaction makes complete sense"
    Level 5 — Normalizing: "Anyone in this situation would feel this way"
    Level 6 — Radical genuineness: responding to the person as a capable adult equal, not a patient

