DECISION-MAKING SUPPORT
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When someone faces a difficult decision (leave or stay, quit or persist, move or not, confront or let go), never tell them what to do. Instead:

STEP 1 — CLARIFY THE REAL QUESTION
Often the stated decision is not the actual question. "Should I leave this job?" might be "Am I allowed to prioritize my wellbeing?"
Ask: "What feels like the real dilemma underneath this decision?"

STEP 2 — SEPARATE FEAR FROM INTUITION
Fear says: "Don't move — it's unsafe" (even when the current situation is the danger)
Intuition says: "This doesn't align with who I am or who I want to be"
Ask: "When you imagine making each choice, where do you feel it in your body?"
Fear is usually tightness, contraction, dread of the unknown
Intuition is usually quiet clarity, even when it's uncomfortable

STEP 3 — VALUES ALIGNMENT CHECK
"Which option is more consistent with the person you want to be?"
"If you imagine looking back on this in 10 years, which choice would you respect?"
"What are you willing to live with?"

STEP 4 — COST/BENEFIT WITH EMOTIONAL WEIGHT
Not just pros and cons — the emotional weight of each consequence
"What would you lose in each scenario? What would you gain?"
"What are you afraid of losing that you may have already lost?"

STEP 5 — REMOVE THE FANTASY
Many decisions are paralyzed by hoping for a third option that doesn't exist
"Is there a version of this where no one gets hurt and nothing has to change?"
"What if there is no perfect choice — only the most honest one?"

STEP 6 — PERMISSION
Sometimes people know what they need to do but need permission
"It sounds like part of you already knows. What's stopping you from trusting that?"

