GROUNDING (panic, dissociation, overwhelm)
- 5-4-3-2-1 sensory: 5 see, 4 touch, 3 hear, 2 smell, 1 taste
- Box breathing: 4 in, 4 hold, 4 out, 4 hold
- Cold water on wrists or face (activates dive reflex, slows heart rate)
- Feet on floor, name 3 physical sensations
- Slow exhale longer than inhale (activates parasympathetic)
- Hum or sigh audibly (vibrates the vagus nerve)
- Physiological sigh: double inhale through nose, long exhale — fastest anxiety reset known

COGNITIVE RESTRUCTURING
- "What evidence do you have for that thought?"
- "What would you tell a dear friend who had this thought?"
- "What's the most realistic outcome — not the worst?"
- Name the distortion, explore alternatives
- "What would I have to believe about myself for this thought to make sense?"

SELF-COMPASSION IN THE MOMENT
- "What would you say to a friend going through exactly this?"
- "Can you offer yourself even a fraction of that kindness right now?"
- Loving-kindness: "May I be safe. May I be at ease. May I be well."
- Self-compassion break (Neff): "This is a moment of suffering. Suffering is part of life. May I be kind to myself in this moment."

INNER CHILD
- "How old does that part of you feel right now?"
- "What did that younger version of you need that they didn't get?"
- "What would you want to say to them now?"
- "What would you have needed to hear then that no one said?"

PARTS WORK (IFS-informed)
- "It sounds like a part of you feels X, while another part feels Y"
- "What is that critical voice trying to protect you from?"
- "Can we get curious about that part instead of fighting it?"
- "If that part of you had a job, what would it be?"
- Parts are not problems — they are protectors with outdated methods

SOMATIC AWARENESS
- "Where do you feel that in your body?"
- "What does it feel like physically — texture, weight, temperature?"
- "If that feeling had something to say, what would it be?"
- "What does your body want to do right now?"
- Notice impulses: move, curl up, push away, reach out — these are nervous system communications

NARRATIVE REFRAMING
- "What story have you been telling yourself about this?"
- "Is that the only story that fits the facts?"
- "What would a story of strength or survival say about this same situation?"
- "What chapter are you actually in — not the whole book?"

EXTERNALIZATION
- "When the Critic shows up, what does it usually say?"
- "When Shame walks in the door, what changes?"
- Naming the problem as separate from the person creates space to examine it

EMOTIONAL LABELING (affect labeling — reduces amygdala activation)
- Name emotions as specifically as possible
- Not just "bad" or "sad" — "disappointed," "humiliated," "hollow," "frantic," "numb"
- The more precise the label, the more regulation occurs neurologically
- "If you had to name five emotions present right now, what would they be?"

