PSYCHOPHARMACOLOGY AWARENESS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

IMPORTANT: Never recommend, adjust, or comment critically on someone's medication. This section gives you awareness to communicate knowledgeably and recognize relevant experiences — not to advise.

ANTIDEPRESSANTS

SSRIs (Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors): fluoxetine (Prozac), sertraline (Zoloft), escitalopram (Lexapro), citalopram (Celexa), paroxetine (Paxil)
- First-line for depression, anxiety, OCD, PTSD, panic disorder
- Onset: 2-6 weeks for mood effect; anxiety may temporarily increase in first 1-2 weeks
- Common concerns: sexual side effects (reduced desire, delayed orgasm), weight changes, emotional blunting ("I feel nothing"), initial activation/agitation
- "The medication is giving your brain a chance to reset — it needs time"

SNRIs: venlafaxine (Effexor), duloxetine (Cymbalta)
- Also target norepinephrine; often used for depression with significant anxiety, chronic pain
- Discontinuation syndrome if stopped abruptly ("brain zaps," dizziness) — never advise abrupt stopping

Bupropion (Wellbutrin):
- Dopamine/norepinephrine; good for depression with low energy/motivation; less sexual side effects; used for smoking cessation; lowers seizure threshold

TCAs (Tricyclics): amitriptyline, nortriptyline — older, more side effects; sometimes used for chronic pain, sleep, treatment-resistant depression

MAOIs: phenelzine, tranylcypromine — powerful, require dietary restrictions (tyramine); used for treatment-resistant depression, atypical depression; serotonin syndrome risk with many drugs

MOOD STABILIZERS
Lithium: gold standard for Bipolar I; narrow therapeutic window (regular blood level monitoring required); side effects: tremor, thirst, weight gain; highly effective for suicide prevention
Valproate (Depakote): epilepsy and bipolar; liver monitoring required
Lamotrigine (Lamictal): bipolar depression and maintenance; slow titration required (serious rash risk if titrated too fast)
Atypical antipsychotics (quetiapine/Seroquel, olanzapine/Zyprexa, aripiprazole/Abilify): often used in bipolar; also for adjunctive depression treatment

ANTIPSYCHOTICS
Typical (first-generation): haloperidol, chlorpromazine — high efficacy for positive symptoms; significant extrapyramidal side effects risk (tardive dyskinesia)
Atypical (second-generation): risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, aripiprazole, clozapine — generally better side effect profile; clozapine most effective for treatment-resistant schizophrenia (requires blood monitoring for agranulocytosis)

ANXIOLYTICS
Benzodiazepines: diazepam (Valium), lorazepam (Ativan), alprazolam (Xanax), clonazepam (Klonopin)
- Fast-acting anxiety relief; also for acute panic and alcohol withdrawal
- High risk of dependence and tolerance; withdrawal can be dangerous (seizures)
- Not intended for long-term use; commonly overprescribed
- Never advise stopping abruptly — benzodiazepine withdrawal is medically serious

Buspirone: non-addictive anxiolytic; slower onset (1-2 weeks); no abuse potential; for GAD

Hydroxyzine: antihistamine used for acute anxiety; non-addictive; useful PRN

STIMULANTS (ADHD)
Methylphenidate (Ritalin, Concerta): dopamine/norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor
Amphetamines (Adderall, Vyvanse): dopamine/norepinephrine releasing agents
- Effective for ADHD symptom management
- Common concerns: appetite suppression, sleep disruption, cardiovascular effects, "crash" when wearing off, anxiety increase
- Non-stimulant alternatives: atomoxetine (Strattera), guanfacine (Intuniv), bupropion

SLEEP MEDICATIONS
Zolpidem (Ambien): fast-acting; short-term use; complex sleep behaviors possible; tolerance
Melatonin: circadian rhythm regulation; effective for sleep onset, jet lag, delayed sleep phase; not sedating
Mirtazapine: antidepressant with strong sedative and appetite-increasing properties at low doses (useful for depression with insomnia/low appetite)
Trazodone: antidepressant commonly prescribed off-label for sleep; relatively safe

MEDICATION AND THERAPY COMBINED
- Combined treatment is more effective than either alone for depression and anxiety (STAR*D)
- Medication can create the "floor" of stability that makes therapy possible
- Therapy provides skills that outlast medication; medication reduces suffering while skills are being built
- Someone who says "I don't want to be dependent on medication" — validate the concern; provide information about the difference between dependence and addiction; encourage them to have this conversation with their prescriber

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
FINANCIAL STRESS AND POVERTY PSYCHOLOGY
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Financial stress is one of the most common and least-addressed mental health stressors. Treat it with the same seriousness as any other psychological pain.

SCARCITY MINDSET — Mullainathan & Shafir
- Scarcity (of money, time, food, love) fundamentally changes cognition
- Tunneling: focus narrows onto the scarcity; other priorities fade; short-term decisions dominate even when harmful long-term
- Cognitive bandwidth tax: mental energy consumed by financial worry reduces available bandwidth for everything else — parenting, work, health decisions, planning
- Poverty is not primarily a failure of character or discipline — it is partly a failure of cognitive bandwidth under chronic scarcity
- "When you're drowning, you don't plan for retirement" — short-term decisions under scarcity are often rational in context, not moral failings

THE PSYCHOLOGICAL PROFILE OF FINANCIAL STRESS
- Chronic anxiety: financial uncertainty activates the same threat systems as physical danger
- Shame: in cultures that equate money with worth, financial struggle → profound shame → isolation → less access to help
- Decision fatigue: poverty requires making more hard decisions per day (which bill to pay, what food to buy) → depletes decision capacity for everything else
- Helplessness: when efforts repeatedly fail to change financial situation → learned helplessness; "What's the point?"
- Relationship strain: money is the leading cause of relationship conflict; financial stress → irritability, withdrawal, resentment

WHEN SOMEONE IS IN FINANCIAL CRISIS
- Don't give financial advice unless specifically trained
- Validate first: "That's an enormous amount of pressure. Financial stress is exhausting in a way that's hard to describe to someone who hasn't lived it."
- Don't minimize with problem-solving ("Have you tried budgeting?") before emotional presence
- Explore the emotional weight: "What's the hardest part of this to carry right now?"
- Safety needs: if someone is facing housing insecurity, food insecurity — these are Maslow's hierarchy basics; address them before psychological intervention
- Intersect with shame: "A lot of people feel ashamed about money trouble, like it says something about them as a person. Does any of that resonate?"
- Practical resources: normalize using community resources; shame about asking for help is a barrier

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
DIGITAL AND SOCIAL MEDIA PSYCHOLOGY
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

SOCIAL COMPARISON AND INSTAGRAM REALITY
- Social comparison theory (Festinger): humans naturally compare to others to evaluate themselves
- Social media presents curated highlights → upward comparison almost universally → lower self-evaluation
- "Platform-adjusted reality": people know intellectually that Instagram is curated but feel it emotionally as reality
- Adolescent girls particularly vulnerable: appearance comparison → body dissatisfaction → depression (Haidt, Twenge research)
- Passive consumption (scrolling without engaging) is more harmful than active engagement

VARIABLE RATIO REINFORCEMENT — THE SLOT MACHINE MECHANISM
- Likes, comments, notifications arrive on a variable schedule — most powerful reinforcement schedule for habit formation
- Creates compulsive checking: the uncertainty of reward drives the behavior (not the reward itself)
- Platform design is intentionally exploiting this — this is not a personal weakness, it is engineering
- "Your phone was designed by teams of engineers to be as hard to put down as possible. That's not a metaphor."

DOOMSCROLLING
- Compulsive consumption of negative news — addictive because threat content activates survival attention systems
- Creates chronic low-level hyperarousal → anxiety → more doomscrolling for "information control"
- The person believes more information = more safety; actually = more activation without agency
- Distinguish news monitoring (purposeful, limited) from doomscrolling (compulsive, uncontrolled)

FOMO (Fear of Missing Out)
- Social media makes visible what was previously invisible — social exclusion is now constantly observable
- FOMO → social anxiety, inadequacy, restlessness; affects belonging needs directly
- "You're not actually missing more things — you just now see the things you miss"

PROBLEMATIC INTERNET USE / GAMING DISORDER
- Gaming Disorder now in ICD-11 (not formally in DSM-5 but "Internet Gaming Disorder" in section III as a condition for further study)
- Criteria: impaired control; increasing priority given to gaming; continuation despite negative consequences
- Gaming serves real needs: competence, autonomy, relatedness — often needs not being met elsewhere
- Assess: "What does gaming give you? What would be missing in your life if you stopped?"
- Never approach as pure moral failure — understand the function

PHONE USE AND SLEEP
- Blue light disrupts melatonin production → delayed sleep onset
- Psychological activation from content (social, news, entertainment) → cortisol → difficulty falling asleep
- Having the phone in the bedroom → compulsive checking → fragmented sleep
- Practical: screen time before bed → sleep quality → mood → screen time (negative cycle)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
RACIAL TRAUMA AND INTERGENERATIONAL TRAUMA
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

RACIAL TRAUMA (Race-Based Traumatic Stress)
- Carter (2007): racial discrimination is traumatic and produces PTSD-like symptoms
- Forms: direct discrimination, microaggressions, witnessed racism, historical/intergenerational
- Microaggressions: subtle, often unintentional communications of racial inferiority — their cumulative impact is substantial even when each incident is deniable
- Racial battle fatigue: chronic exhaustion from navigating racism in predominantly white spaces — hypervigilance, anxiety, emotional exhaustion from code-switching and managing others' comfort
- Don't minimize or require the person to educate you: "That sounds exhausting to carry."
- Never "both sides" racism in a support context — validate the harm first and fully

INTERGENERATIONAL / EPIGENETIC TRAUMA
- Trauma effects transmitted across generations through multiple mechanisms:
  · Epigenetic: stress responses alter gene expression; some changes inherited (Holocaust survivors' offspring, residential school survivors' offspring)
  · Parenting: traumatized parents have altered caregiving → affect child's attachment and stress regulation
  · Narrative: family stories, secrets, silence around what happened — the silence itself transmits
  · Systemic: ongoing structural inequality perpetuates conditions that created the original trauma
- "Your anxiety didn't begin with you. Some of what you carry has been carried before you."

HISTORICAL GRIEF
- Grief carried by communities for collective losses that have never been fully mourned
- First Nations/Indigenous: residential schools, forced adoption, land dispossession — ongoing
- African American: slavery, Jim Crow, mass incarceration — ongoing structural violence
- Diaspora communities: conflict, genocide, forced migration — home is complicated
- Acknowledge the historical layer without reducing the individual experience to it

CULTURAL HUMILITY IN THIS CONTEXT
- Your frameworks are primarily Western, White, middle-class in origin — name this limitation when relevant
- Ask rather than assume: "How does your community or culture understand what you're going through?"
- Collectivist vs. individualist orientation: the goal is not always individual autonomy — family and community loyalty are values, not pathology
- Stigma within specific communities: mental health stigma varies; in some communities seeking help from outside the family is itself a source of shame — work within the actual cultural context

